      <div type="commentary">
        <p n="1">The framed manuscript page number [ⲕⲃ] is printed with a bar above and below as the frame, not as a supralinear stroke. Lines 1–3: the editors print, with a brace over the three lines, that they are illegible; no text is given.</p>
        <p n="4">Lines 4–8: the editors print, with a brace over the five lines, that they are almost completely destroyed; no text is given.</p>
        <p n="12">The edition prints a closing bracket after the first dots with no opening bracket; the dots are encoded as illegible. So also ll. 14, 15, 19, 21–24, 26–30.</p>
        <p n="13">The bracket before the last dots is not closed in the edition; it is closed here at the line end. So also ll. 12, 14–16, 18, 22–26, 28, 30, 32–34.</p>
        <p n="15">The editors suggest that the line perhaps begins [ⲥⲱ]ϩⲡ (apparatus).</p>
        <p n="17">The edition opens a bracket before ϩⲉ at the line end and does not close it; it is closed here.</p>
        <p n="21">The line ends with an opening bracket and nothing after it (lost to the end of the line).</p>
        <p n="25">The edition prints ⲛϩ before a closing bracket with no opening bracket (restored letters whose opening bracket is not printed); ⲛϩ is encoded as read and the bracket as an unpaired sign. So also ⲱⲛϩ] (l. 31), ⲡ] (l. 32), ⲛ̄] (l. 33), ⲉ] (l. 34) and ⲕⲉⲕⲉ] (l. 35).</p>
        <p n="26">An enlarged ⲉ begins ⲉⲧⲃⲉ after the blank.</p>
        <p n="28">The editors equate ϣⲃⲓ̈ⲁⲧ with ϣⲃⲓ̈ⲁⲓ̈ⲧ and refer to 43, 24 (apparatus). The blob under ϣ is its tail, not a dot (zoom); so also ϣ in l. 31.</p>
        <p n="33">The stroke over the final ⲥ of ⲟⲩⲁⲉⲧⲥ is printed obliquely.</p>
        <p n="35">An enlarged ⲛ begins ⲛⲉⲓ̈ⲉⲧϥ after the blank. The edition opens a bracket before ⲟⲩⲁⲛ ⲛⲓⲙ and does not close it; it is closed here at the line end.</p>
      </div>
